# /etc/sudoers.d/office — scoped sudo for the AI Agent Office orchestrator.
#
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE AND MUST BE REGENERATED PER HOST.
# The account names below are the ones the design's example uses. Whether
# they exist on the target VPS, and which department each owns, is an open
# question on ticket 007 and on the design itself ("Who owns which
# department by default?"). Do not install this file unmodified onto a host
# whose accounts you have not checked with `id <user>`.
#
# What this grants, in one sentence: the unprivileged `office` account —
# which the orchestrator runs as — may run /usr/bin/tmux, and only
# /usr/bin/tmux, as each agent account, without a password.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Install and verify (run these on the VPS, as root):
#
#   visudo -c -f ./office                 # syntax check BEFORE installing
#   install -o root -g root -m 0440 ./office /etc/sudoers.d/office
#   visudo -c                             # re-check the whole sudoers set
#   sudo -u office sudo -n -l             # what office may actually do
#   sudo -u office sudo -n -u gluy /usr/bin/tmux -V   # must print a version
#
# `visudo -c -f` is the acceptance check and it is deliberately the first
# step: a sudoers file with a syntax error can lock every user out of sudo,
# and /etc/sudoers.d files are parsed as part of the whole set. Mode 0440
# and root ownership are not decoration — sudo refuses to read a file in
# sudoers.d that is group- or world-writable, and does so silently enough
# that the symptom looks like "the rule does nothing".
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes that are decisions, not commentary:
#
# * The path must match byte for byte what the orchestrator executes. sudo
#   matches the permitted command as a literal path, so /usr/bin/tmux and
#   /bin/tmux are two different grants even on a host where one is a symlink
#   to the other. `TMUX_BIN` in .env must equal the path written here; check
#   with `command -v tmux` on the target host.
#
# * The orchestrator always calls sudo with -n (non-interactive). A rule
#   that still demands a password therefore fails immediately and visibly
#   instead of blocking on a prompt no human will ever see. Keep NOPASSWD,
#   or every spawn fails with "a password is required".
#
# * No env_keep, and none is needed. Environment for an agent session is set
#   by `tmux new-session -e KEY=VALUE`, not by exporting it through sudo, so
#   sudo's env_reset default stays untouched and CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR /
#   NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS still reach the pane process.
#
# * DELIBERATE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT: granting /usr/bin/tmux in full lets
#   `office` run `tmux new-session` with an ARBITRARY command as any of the
#   accounts below. This is not a sandbox; it is a grant of shell-equivalent
#   access to those three accounts, and the design accepts it. Narrowing it
#   would require a wrapper script that fixes the command line and grants
#   only the wrapper — worth doing when the orchestrator stops being trusted
#   code, and a change that belongs with a ticket that can test it.
#
# * The orchestrator never runs as root and this file never grants (root).
#   The adapter refuses to start if its own uid is 0, so a misconfigured
#   service unit fails loudly instead of quietly gaining the world.
#
# * Anything added here must also be added to TMUX_AGENT_USERS in .env. The
#   adapter checks that allowlist before spawning and will refuse an account
#   this file grants but the config does not know about. Two lists is
#   deliberate: sudo is the kernel-side boundary, TMUX_AGENT_USERS is the
#   application-side one, and neither is trusted to be the only check.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Dev department account. The orchestrator drives this user's agent desks.
office ALL=(gluy)  NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tmux

# Second team account (placeholder name from the design's example).
office ALL=(user2) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tmux

# Third team account (placeholder name from the design's example).
office ALL=(user3) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tmux
